F,T\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle(BT,\\,BE)=\\angle(FT,\\,FE). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Adding (4) to (6) and employing (2) (which gives \\(\\angle(PH,\\,MN)=\\angle(PM,\\,PN)\\) and therefore \\(\\angle(\\ell ,\\,MN)=\\angle(PM,\\,PN)\\) because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is symmetric to \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) with respect to the line \\(MN\\)) yields (5).  \n\n   Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\).\n\nSince the only point of \\(\\ell\\) that lies on \\(\\omega\\) is \\(T\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) with the order \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);  \n* \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\);  \n* \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega\\).\n\nWe shall prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity\n\\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) which sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B .\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nFor any two lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\) passing through \\(A\\) the oriented\nangle is preserved by \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\); in particular\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell_{1},AE)=\\angle(\\ell_{1},AF). \\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(\\ell_{1}=AP\\) we obtain the crucial equality\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF)\\;}. \\tag{1.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) orthogonal\nto \\(MN\\); denote its foot by \\(Q\\).  Since \\(H\\) is the intersection of the\naltitudes, the line \\(PH\\) coincides with \\(PQ\\) and therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle(PH,MN)=\\angle(PM,PN). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(MN\\) equals\nthe angle that \\(AP\\) makes with \\(MN\\).  Using (2.1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle(PM,PN)\\;}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Equality of the angles that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\)\n\nFrom (1.3) and the parallelism \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) we immediately get\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle(\\ell ,BF)\\;}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The point of tangency\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle\n\\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Because \\(B,E,F,T\\) are concyclic, the\ninscribed\u2011angle theorem yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle(BT,BE)=\\angle(FT,FE). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nAdding (3.1) to (4.1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,BT)=\\angle(\\ell ,FT). \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4.2) is precisely the tangency criterion: a line through a point\n\\(T\\) on a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes equal angles with the\ntwo chords through \\(T\\).  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point\n\\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to\n\\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a single point \\(T\\) and,\nby (4.2), is tangent to that circle at \\(T\\).  This completes the proof.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
