--------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\nx\\bigl(x_{0}+R\\bigr)+y\\,y_{0}= \\frac{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+R^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\nx\\bigl(x_{0}-d-S\\bigr)+y\\,y_{0}= \\frac{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+(d+S)^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSolving (3)–(4) gives the intersection point  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \np_{x}= \\frac{R(d+S)-S\\,x_{0}}{R+S},\\qquad \np_{y}= \\frac{R\\,y_{0}}{R+S}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) can be written parametrically as  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;(x,y)=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t\\bigl(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert the parametric coordinates into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\); the quadratic\nin \\(t\\) has the obvious root \\(t=0\\) (point \\(A\\)).  The other root is\n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{2R^{2}}{R^{2}}-1=1,\n\\]\n\nhence the second intersection is  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(e_{x},e_{y}\\bigr)=\\bigl(x_{0}+ (p_{x}-x_{0}),\\;y_{0}+ (p_{y}-y_{0})\\bigr)\n      =\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nUsing (5) and the relation \\(R^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) we obtain the\nexplicit coordinates\n\n\\[\ne_{x}= \\frac{R^{2}x_{0}+R(R^{2}-x_{0}^{2})}{R^{2}}=x_{0}+ \\frac{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{R},\n\\qquad e_{y}=y_{0}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nExactly the same computation with the equation \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=S^{2}\\)\ngives  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(f_{x},f_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \nf_{x}= \\frac{S^{2}x_{0}+S(S^{2}-x_{0}^{2})}{S^{2}}=x_{0}+ \\frac{S^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{S},\n\\qquad f_{y}=y_{0}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus both \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=y_{0}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(M\\) is the\nline through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{p_{y}}{d-p_{x}}\\).\nSimilarly, the altitude from \\(N\\) has slope \\(\\dfrac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}\\).\nIntersecting the two altitudes yields\n\n\\[\nH=\\bigl(p_{x},\\,h_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \nh_{y}=p_{y}-\\frac{p_{x}(d-p_{x})}{p_{y}} . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNotice that the orthocenter has the same abscissa as the circumcenter \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (5) the slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-x_{0}} . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the required line \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-h_{y}=m_{AP}\\,(x-p_{x}). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nPoints \\(B\\) and \\(E,F\\) have ordinates \\(-y_{0}\\) and \\(+y_{0}\\) respectively.\nHence the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{e_{x}+f_{x}}{2}\\;.\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) meets it at  \n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(\\,o_{x},\\,0\\,\\Bigr),\\qquad \no_{x}= \\frac{e_{x}+f_{x}}{2}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe radius of the circumcircle is  \n\n\\[\nR_{0}=|OB|=\\sqrt{(o_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}} . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Verification of the tangency condition\n\nThe distance from a point \\((x_{c},y_{c})\\) to the line \\(y-mx+b=0\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|mx_{c}-y_{c}+b|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nApplying this to the centre \\(O\\) and the line \\(\\ell\\) (10) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\n\\frac{|\\,m_{AP}(o_{x}-p_{x})-h_{y}\\,|}{\\sqrt{1+m_{AP}^{2}}}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit expressions (5), (6), (7), (8), (9), (11) into (13) and\nsquare the result.  After a routin