f $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###\n**a. Verdict** – I present a new proof.  \nThe statement of the problem is true; the previous attempt failed because it relied on an unjustified claim that the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\).  The new proof avoids any such assumption and uses only elementary angle‑chasing together with the well‑known facts that  \n\n* the line joining the two intersection points of two circles is perpendicular to the line of their centres, and  \n* the orthocenter of a triangle together with the three vertices form a set of four points whose opposite angles are supplementary.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. Show that \\(AB\\perp MN\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\).  \n2. Prove that the quadrilateral \\(B\\,F\\,H\\,P\\) is cyclic.  \n3. From the cyclicity obtain \\(\\angle BFH=\\angle BPH\\).  \n4. Use the parallelism \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) to replace \\(\\angle BPH\\) by \\(\\angle BAP\\).  \n5. Conclude that \\(\\angle BFH=\\angle BAP\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes equal angles with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), i.e. it is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively,  \n* \\(A,B\\) their intersection points,  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles (the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\)),  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\),  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\),  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\((X)\\) the circumcircle of a set of points \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Preliminary perpendicularities  \n\nBecause the two circles intersect, the line joining their intersection points is the radical axis of the circles; hence  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(the radical axis is perpendicular to the line of centres).\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by (1) this altitude is parallel to \\(AB\\).  \nSince the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on every altitude, we have  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle HPA\\qquad\\text{(both are the angle between }AB\\text{ and }AP).\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,F\\,H\\,P\\) is cyclic  \n\nObserve first that the points \\(C,D,P\\) are concyclic by definition of \\(P\\); denote this circle by \\((ACD)\\).  \nBecause \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) is \\(PH\\) and the altitude from \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) is the line through \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) perpendicular to \\(PN\\) (resp. \\(PM\\)).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPH=\\angle DPH=90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nHence the four points \\(C,D,H,P\\) lie on a circle with diameter \\(PH\\); denote this circle by \\((CDH)\\).\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((CDH)\\) contain the chord \\(CD\\).  \nThe centres of the two circles lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore the two circles are symmetric with respect to that perpendicular bisector.  \nConsequently the arcs subtended by the same chord are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\widehat{AC}=\\widehat{HC},\\qquad \\widehat{AD}=\\widehat{HD}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the points \\(B\\) and \\(F\\).  \nSince \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\((ACD)\\) equals the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\((CDH)\\); indeed\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(ACD)}(B)&=BA\\cdot BB_{\\,\\Omega}=BA\\cdot BB_{\\,\\Gamma},\\\\\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(CDH)}(B)&=BC\\cdot BD,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nand the equalities \\(BA\\cdot BB_{\\,\\Omega}=BC\\cdot BD\\) follow from the intersecting‑chords theorem applied to the two original circles (the chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) intersect at the point \\(X\\) where the line \\(MN\\) meets the radical axis; the theorem gives \\(AB\\cdot BB_{\\,\\Omega}=CB\\cdot DB\\)).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nBA\\cdot BB_{\\,\\Omega}=BC\\cdot BD.\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(F\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\) and on \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(F\\) with respect to \\((CDH)\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(CDH)}(F)=FC\\cdot FD.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(F\\) also belongs to \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(F)=FA\\cdot FF_{\\,\\Gamma}=FC\\cdot FD.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nFA\\cdot FF_{\\,\\Gamma}=FC\\cdot FD.\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BA}{BC}= \\frac{FA}{FC},\\qquad \n\\frac{BB_{\\,\\Omega}}{BD}= \\frac{FF_{\\,\\Gamma}}{FD}.\n\\]\n\nHence the pairs of directed segments \\((B,A)\\) and \\((F,A)\\) are in the same ratio as the pairs \\((B,C)\\) and \\((F,C)\\); this is pre