\n\nFinally, from (3) we have \\(\\angle BPA=\\angle BAP\\).  Combining (8), (9) and (3) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BFH=\\angle BAP\\;}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Tangency  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) equals \\(\\angle BAP\\).  \nEquation (10) tells us that this angle also equals \\(\\angle BFH\\), i.e. the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the other chord \\(BF\\).  \n\nHence \\(\\ell\\) makes equal angles with the two chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) of the same circle; consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the unique point where it meets that circle.  \n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎"
