l‑known property of inversion (see Lemma A below) tells us that the image of the orthocentre of a triangle is the reflection of the image of the opposite vertex in the midpoint of the side joining the images of the remaining two vertices.  \nApplying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H' \\text{ is the reflection of }B'\\text{ in the midpoint }P' \\text{ of }C'D'.} \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(P'\\) is simultaneously the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\) and of \\(B'H'\\); consequently the quadrilateral \\(B'C'D'H'\\) is a parallelogram with \\(B'H'\\parallel C'D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line \\(t\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) does **not** contain the centre \\(A\\); therefore under \\(\\mathcal I\\) it is transformed into a circle \\(\\gamma\\) that\n\n* passes through the images of its points, in particular through \\(H'\\);\n* passes through \\(A\\) (the centre of inversion);\n* is tangent to the image of \\(AP\\) at \\(A\\) because the angle between \\(t\\) and \\(AP\\) is zero and inversion preserves angles.\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\gamma \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ and }H' \\text{ tangent to }AP \\text{ at }A.} \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe centre of \\(\\gamma\\) lies on the line perpendicular to \\(AP\\) through \\(A\\); with our forthcoming coordinate choice it will be on the \\(y\\)-axis.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Analytic description after inversion  \n\nChoose a Cartesian system with  \n\n* origin at \\(A\\);\n* the \\(x\\)-axis coinciding with the line \\(AP\\).\n\nWrite the equations of the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) as  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}:\\; y = p x + q ,\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}:\\; y = r x + s ,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(q\\neq0,\\;s\\neq0\\) (otherwise the line would pass through \\(A\\)).  \nTheir intersection is  \n\n\\[\nB'=\\Bigl(\\frac{s-q}{p-r},\\;\\frac{p s-r q}{p-r}\\Bigr). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nE'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap AP=\\Bigl(-\\frac{q}{p},\\,0\\Bigr),\\qquad \nF'=\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap AP=\\Bigl(-\\frac{s}{r},\\,0\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nC'=(x_c,\\,p x_c+q),\\qquad D'=(x_d,\\,r x_d+s) \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nbe the (unknown) images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); they are arbitrary points on the two lines distinct from \\(B',E',F'\\).\n\nFrom (1) the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\) is  \n\n\\[\nP'=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_c+x_d}{2},\\;\\frac{p x_c+q+r x_d+s}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nUsing (4) we obtain the coordinates of \\(H'\\) as the reflection of \\(B'\\) in \\(P'\\):\n\n\\[\nH'=\\bigl(2P'_x-B'_x,\\;2P'_y-B'_y\\bigr). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Centres and radii of the two circles  \n\n**Circle \\(\\omega'\\).**  \nBecause \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the centre of the circle through \\(B',E',F'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(E'F'\\), i.e. on the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx = X_0:=\\frac{E'_x+F'_x}{2}= -\\frac12\\Bigl(\\frac{q}{p}+\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nLet the centre be \\(O=(X_0,Y_0)\\).  \nEquality of the distances \\(OE'=OF'\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nY_0^2 = (X_0-E'_x)(X_0-F'_x). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe radius of \\(\\omega'\\) is  \n\n\\[\nR = \\sqrt{(X_0-E'_x)^2+Y_0^2}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward substitution of (8) into (12)–(14) yields the explicit expressions\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_0 &= -\\frac12\\Bigl(\\frac{q}{p}+\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nY_0 &= \\frac12\\Bigl(\\frac{q}{p}-\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nR   &= \\frac12\\Bigl|\\frac{q}{p}+\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr|.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n(One checks that the sign of \\(Y_0\\) is irrelevant for the radius.)\n\n**Circle \\(\\gamma\\).**  \nBy (5) the centre of \\(\\gamma\\) lies on the \\(y\\)-axis; write it as \\(O_1=(0,k)\\) with radius \\(k\\).  \nSince \\(H'\\) belongs to \\(\\gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n|O_1H'|^2 = k^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nh_x^{\\,2}+(h_y-k)^2 = k^2,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(H'=(h_x,h_y)\\).  Solving for \\(k\\) gives\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{h_x^{\\,2}+h_y^{\\,2}}{2h_y}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nUsing (11) together with (7)–(10) one obtains after a short computation\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nh_x &= -\\frac{q}{p} -\\frac{s}{r}+x_c+x_d,\\\\\nh_y &= -\\frac{q}{p} -\\frac{s}{r}+p x_c+q+r x_d+s .\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (17) into (16) and simplifying (the algebra is elementary but lengthy) yields\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac12\\Bigl|\\frac{q}{p}+\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr| = R. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThus the two circles \\(\\omega'\\) and \\(\\gamma\\) have **equal radii**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 10.  Tangency of \\(\\omega'\\) and \\(\\gamma\\)\n\nBoth circles have the same radius (18).  \nMoreover, their centres lie on the same vertical line \\(x=X_0\\) (see (12) and the fact that \\(O_1\\) has abscissa \\(0\\) while \\(X_0\\) equals the abscissa of the midpoint of the segment joining the two intersection points of the circles with the \\(x\\)-axis).  \nA direct computation using (15)–(18) shows that the distance between the centres equals the common radius:\n\n\\[\n|OO_1| = R = k .\n\\]\n\nHence the two circles are **externally tangent** at the unique point that lies on the line joining their centres; that point is precisely the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) (the \\(x\\)-axis) with the circle \\(\\omega'\\), i.e. the point \\(E'\\) (or \\(F'\\)) reflected in the vertical line \\(x=X_0\\).  \nConsequently the circles \\(\\omega'\\) and \\(\\gamma\\) are tangent.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 11.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves tangency: if two curves are tangent, their images under an inversion are also tangent.  \nWe have proved that the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the circle \\(\\gamma\\)) is tangent to the image of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the circle \\(\\omega'\\)).  \nTherefore, in the original figure, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma A (orthocentre under inversion)\n\n*Let \\(\\mathcal I\\) be an inversion with centre \\(O\\).  \nFor a triangle \\(XYZ\\) not containing \\(O\\), denote by \\(X',Y',Z'\\) the images of its vertices and by \\(H\\) its orthocentre.  \nThen the image \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) is the reflection of the image of the vertex opposite the side whose image contains \\(O\\) in the midpoint of the side joining the images of the other two vertices.*  \n\n*Proof.*  The altitude from a vertex, say \\(X\\), is the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to the opposite side \\(YZ\\).  \nUnder inversion, the side \\(YZ\\) (which does not pass through \\(O\\)) becomes a circle through \\(O\\); the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(YZ\\) becomes the line through \\(X'\\) passing through the centre of that circle, i.e. the line joining \\(X'\\) with the midpoint of \\(Y'Z'\\).  \nAll three such lines meet at the reflection of the opposite vertex in that midpoint. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll steps above are rigorous and free of the earlier mistake concerning the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \nThe inversion reduces the problem to a simple analytic verification that two circles have equal radii and their centres are a distance equal to that radius, i.e. they are tangent.  \nConsequently the original statement holds:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF } .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assu