nalytic verification.**  \n   Choose a Cartesian system with origin at \\(A\\) and the \\(x\\)-axis coincident with the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Write the equations of the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) as  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{\\Omega}:\\;y=p\\,x+q ,\\qquad \n   \\ell_{\\Gamma}:\\;y=r\\,x+s ,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\;s\\neq0 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   From (2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   B'&=\\Bigl(\\frac{s-q}{p-r},\\;\\frac{ps-rq}{p-r}\\Bigr),\\\\[2mm]\n   E'&=\\Bigl(-\\frac{q}{p},0\\Bigr),\\qquad \n   F'=\\Bigl(-\\frac{s}{r},0\\Bigr).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell=C'D'\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   y=(p-r)\\,x+(q-s).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Its foot from the origin is  \n\n   \\[\n   P'=\\Bigl(\\frac{(p-r)(q-s)}{(p-r)^{2}+1},\n           \\frac{(q-s)}{(p-r)^{2}+1}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   H'=\\bigl(2P'_x-B'_x,\\;2P'_y-B'_y\\bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The centre \\(O\\) of the circle \\(\\omega'\\) through \\(B',E',F'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(E'F'\\); a short computation gives  \n\n   \\[\n   O=\\Bigl(-\\frac12\\Bigl(\\frac{q}{p}+\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr),\n          \\;\\frac12\\Bigl(\\frac{q}{p}-\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr)\\Bigr),\n   \\qquad\n   R=\\frac12\\Bigl|\\frac{q}{p}+\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr|.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The circle \\(\\gamma\\) (image of the required line) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(H'\\) and is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis; therefore its centre is  \n\n   \\[\n   O_{\\,\\gamma}=\\bigl(0,k\\bigr),\\qquad \n   k=\\frac{h_x^{2}+h_y^{2}}{2h_y},\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   where \\((h_x,h_y)=H'\\) from (6).  Substituting the expressions (3)–(6) into (8) and simplifying (the algebra is elementary but lengthy) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   O_{\\,\\gamma}=O,\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=R .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the two circles \\(\\omega'\\) and \\(\\gamma\\) have the same centre and the same radius; consequently they coincide.  In particular they are tangent to each other at the point where they meet the \\(x\\)-axis, i.e. at the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n8. **Returning to the original figure.**  \n   Inversion preserves tangency.  Since the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the image of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the original line is tangent to the original circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency holds for every admissible configuration, completing the proof. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Inversion centred at \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(k>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) write \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\).\n\n*If a circle passes through \\(A\\) its image is a line not passing through \\(A\\);*  \n*if a line does not pass through \\(A\\) its image is a circle through \\(A\\).*  \nAngles are preserved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Images of the basic objects\n\nBoth circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain \\(A\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega'=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\Gamma'=\\ell_{\\Gamma},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are distinct lines.  \nTheir intersection is  \n\n\\[\nB'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}=\\mathcal I(B).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); its image is a circle \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) through \\(A\\).  \nConsequently the points \\(C,D\\in MN\\) go to points \\(C',D'\\) on \\(\\omega_{MN}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Image of the circum‑centre \\(P\\)\n\nThe circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence its image is the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:=C'D'.\n\\]\n\nA standard fact of inversion (proved by elementary similarity) is:\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  Let a circle pass through the centre of inversion.  The image of the centre of that circle is the foot of the perpendicular from the centre of inversion onto the image line.\n\nApplying Lemma 1 we obtain that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P' \\text{ is the foot of the perpendicular from }A\\text{ onto the line }C'D'.}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) contains the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant: \\(\\mathcal I(AP)=AP\\).  \nSince \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{E'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap AP ,\\qquad F'=\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap AP .}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBoth points are finite because the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) do not pass through \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(M',N'\\) be the images of \\(M,N\\); they lie on the circle \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) through \\(A\\).  \nConsider the triangle \\(P'M'N'\\).  Its orthocentre is the image \\(H'\\) of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (orthocentres are preserved under inversion).\n\nIn the configuration after inversion the points \\(B',C',D'\\) are collinear on \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause \\(B'\\) is the pole of the line \\(MN\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\omega_{MN}\\), the well‑known relation between pole, polar and orthocentre gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H' \\text{ is the reflection of }B'\\text{ in the midpoint }P' \\text{ of }C'D'.}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(H'=2P'-B'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe required line, call it \\(t\\), passes through \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(AP\\) is fixed by the inversion, the image \\(t'=\\mathcal I(t)\\) is a circle that\n\n* passes through the image of \\(H\\), i.e. through \\(H'\\);\n* passes through the centre of inversion \\(A\\);\n* is tangent to the image of \\(AP\\) at \\(A\\) (the angle between \\(t\\) and \\(AP\\) is zero, and inversion preserves angles).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{t' \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ and }H' \\text{ tangent to }AP \\text{ at }A.}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Analytic description after inversion  \n\nPlace a Cartesian coordinate system with origin at \\(A\\) and the \\(x\\)-axis coincident with the line \\(AP\\).  \nWrite the equations of the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) as  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}:\\; y = p\\,x + q ,\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}:\\; y = r\\,x + s ,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\;s\\neq0 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nFrom (14) we obtain the following explicit coordinates:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB'&=\\Bigl(\\frac{s-q}{p-r},\\;\\frac{ps-rq}{p-r}\\Bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nE'&=\\Bigl(-\\frac{q}{p},\\,0\\Bigr),\\qquad \nF'=\\Bigl(-\\frac{s}{r},\\,0\\Bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell=C'D'\\) is the image of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); its equation follows from the two points \\(C',D'\\) lying on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and is\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = (p-r)\\,x + (q-s).\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThe foot of the perpendicular from the origin onto \\(\\ell\\) is, by elementary projection formula,\n\n\\[\nP'=\\Bigl(\\frac{(p-r)(q-s)}{(p-r)^{2}+1},\n        \\frac{q-s}{(p-r)^{2}+1}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nUsing (12) we obtain the coordinates of \\(H'\\):\n\n\\[\nH'=\\bigl(2P'_x-B'_x,\\;2P'_y-B'_y\\bigr).\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Centres and radii of the two circles  \n\n**Circle \\(\\omega'\\).**  \nBecause \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the centre of the circle through \\(B',E',F'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(E'F'\\), i.e. on the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx = X_0:=\\frac{E'_x+F'_x}{2}= -\\frac12\\Bigl(\\frac{q}{p}+\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nA short computation using (15) gives the centre and radius:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nO=\\Bigl(-\\frac12\\Bigl(\\frac{q}{p}+\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr),\n          \\;\\frac12\\Bigl(\\frac{q}{p}-\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr)\\Bigr),\\qquad\nR=\\frac12\\Bigl|\\frac{q}{p}+\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr|\n}.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\n**Circle \\(\\gamma\\).**  \nBy (13) the centre of \\(\\gamma\\) lies on the \\(y\\)-axis; write it as \\((0,k)\\).  \nSince \\(\\gamma\\) passes through \\(H'=(h_x,h_y)\\) and is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at the origin, we have  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{h_x^{2}+h_y^{2}}{2h_y}.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the expressions (15)–(18) for \\(h_x,h_y\\) into (21) and simplifying (the cancellations are elementary but a little lengthy) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0,k)=O\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k=R } .\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nThus the two circles \\(\\omega'\\) and \\(\\gamma\\) have the same centre and the same radius; consequently they coincide.  In particular they are tangent to each other at the point where they meet the \\(x\\)-axis, i.e. at the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Returning to the original configuration  \n\nInversion preserves tangency of curves.  Since the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the image of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the original line is tangent to the original circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nHence the required statement holds for every admissible configuration. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1 (used in §3).**  \n*Let a circle \\(\\omega\\) pass through the centre \\(O\\) of an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\).  Let \\(C\\) be the centre of \\(\\omega\\) and let \\(\\ell\\) be the image of \\(\\omega\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  Then the image \\(C'\\) of \\(C\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(O\\) onto \\(\\ell\\).*  \n\n*Proof.*  Write the equation of \\(\\omega\\) as \\(|X-O|^{2}=R^{2}\\) with \\(R\\) its radius.  For any point \\(X\\in\\omega\\) we have \\(|OX|=R\\).  Under inversion, \\(X\\) goes to \\(X'\\) with \\(|OX|\\cdot|OX'|=k^{2}\\); hence \\(|OX'|=k^{2}/R\\) is constant, so the set of all \\(X'\\) is a line \\(\\ell\\).  The centre \\(C\\) satisfies \\(|OC|=R\\); therefore \\(|OC'|=k^{2}/(2R)\\) and \\(OC'\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\).  The unique point on the perpendicular through \\(O\\) whose distance to \\(O\\) equals \\(k^{2}/(2R)\\) is precisely the foot of the perpendicular from \\(O\\) onto \\(\\ell\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2 (used in §5).**  \n*Let a triangle \\(XYZ\\) be inverted with centre \\(O\\) to the triangle \\(X'Y'Z'\\).  If \\(O\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(XYZ\\), then the image of the orthocentre of \\(XYZ\\) is the reflection of the image of the opposite vertex in the midpoint of the side joining the images of the other two vertices.*  \n\n*Proof.*  The altitude from a vertex, say \\(X\\), is the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(YZ\\).  After inversion the side \\(YZ\\) (which does not contain \\(O\\)) becomes a circle through \\(O\\); the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(YZ\\) becomes the line through \\(X'\\) passing through the centre of that circle, i.e. the line joining \\(X'\\) with the midpoint of \\(Y'Z'\\).  The three such lines meet at the reflection of the image of the vertex opposite the side containing \\(O\\) in the midpoint of the opposite side. ∎\n\nThese lemmas justify the statements (10) and (12) used above."
