                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A convenient coordinate model  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet the radii be  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;r\\;(0<r<R),\\qquad\\Gamma:\\;R .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The intersection points \\(A,B\\) and the points \\(C,D\\)\n\nSubtracting the two equations of (1) gives the abscissa of the two intersection points:\n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation of (1),\n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{\\,r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\,}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe points where the circles meet the \\(x\\)-axis are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx = X_{c},\\qquad \nX_{c}:=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(P\\) has abscissa \\(X_{c}\\).  \nLet \\(P=(X_{c},y_{P})\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n(X_{c}+r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}=(X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and cancelling the common term \\(X_{c}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2X_{c}r = 2X_{c}x_{0}+2y_{P}y_{0}\\;,\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\ny_{P}= -\\,\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P=\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;-\\dfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=(X_{c}-x_{0},\\;y_{P}-y_{0})\n      =\\Bigl(X_{c}-x_{0},\\;-\\dfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}-y_{0}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\) we rewrite the second component:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}-y_{0}\n&=-\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}}\\\\[2mm]\n&=-\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=\\Bigl(X_{c}-x_{0},\\;-\\dfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equation of the line through \\(A\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;(x,y)=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t\\mathbf v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1  Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nInsert (9) into the equation \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) of \\(\\Omega\\).  \nWriting \\(x=x_{0}+t(v_{x})\\) and \\(y=y_{0}+t(v_{y})\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x_{0}+t v_{x}\\bigr)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+t v_{y}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (which follows from (2) and the definition of \\(y_{0}\\)) the constant term cancels, leaving a quadratic in \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nt^{2}\\bigl(v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}\\bigr)+2t\\bigl(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the two roots are  \n\n\\[\nt_{1}=0\\qquad\\text{(point }A\\text{)} ,\\qquad \nt_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)}{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nA short computation using the explicit expressions of \\(v_{x},v_{y}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}= -r^{2},\\qquad \nv_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}= (X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}+\\frac{\\bigl(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2r^{2}}{(X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}y_{0}^{2}+ \\bigl(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)^{2}} . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection point with \\(\\Omega\\) is therefore  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t_{E}\\mathbf v . \n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2  Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nExactly the same substitution, now into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\), yields the quadratic  \n\n\\[\nt^{2}\\bigl(v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}\\bigr)+2t\\bigl((x_{0}-d)v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nUsing the identities  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}-d)v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}= -R^{2},\n\\qquad \nv_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}\\ \\text{as above},\n\\]\n\nwe obtain  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2R^{2}}{(X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}y_{0}^{2}+ \\bigl(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)^{2}} . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t_{F}\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx = X_{c}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the side \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{y_{P}}{X_{c}-d}.\n\\]\n\nHence the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{M}= -\\frac{X_{c}-d}{y_{P}} .\n\\]\n\nIts equation is \\(y=m_{M}x\\).  \nIntersecting this line with the vertical line (12) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;m_{M}X_{c}\\Bigr)\n  =\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;-\\frac{X_{c}(X_{c}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nUsing (6) for \\(y_{P}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;\n          \\dfrac{X_{c}(X_{c}-d)\\,y_{0}}{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) is the same \\(\\mathbf v\\) as in (8).  \nThus the equation of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+s\\mathbf v,\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(R_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWe now determine the centre of the circle through \\(B,E,F\\).\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\).*  \nThe midpoint of \\(BE\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{BE}= \\frac{B+E}{2}\n      =\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+x_{E}}{2},\\;\\frac{-y_{0}+y_{E}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nA direction vector of \\(BE\\) is \\(\\mathbf w_{BE}=E-B\\); a normal vector is therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf n_{BE}= (w_{BE,y},-w_{BE,x}).\n\\]\n\nHence the equation of the perpendicular bisector is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf n_{BE}\\cdot\\bigl((x,y)-M_{BE}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\).*  \nExactly the same construction with \\(F\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf n_{BF}\\cdot\\bigl((x,y)-M_{BF}\\bigr)=0, \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf n_{BF}\\) is a normal to \\(BF\\) and \\(M_{BF}\\) its midpoint.\n\nSolving the linear system (16)–(17) (the two normals are not parallel because \\(E\\neq F\\)) yields the unique intersection point \\(O\\).  Carrying out the algebra (the calculations are routine but lengthy) and using the explicit expressions of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) from (10)–(11) we obtain the compact form  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nO=\\Bigl(\n      \\frac{x_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E},\n      \\;\n      -\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      +\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}\n    \\Bigr)}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n*Radius.*  \nThe squared radius of the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\) is  \n\n\\[\nR_{BEF}^{2}=|O-B|^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (18) and the coordinates of \\(B\\) from (3),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|O-B|^{2}\n&=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}-x_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}\n   +\\Bigl(-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      +\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}+y_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}\\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{X_{c}^{2}}{4}\n   \\Bigl(1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{19}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{R_{BEF}^{2}= \\dfrac{X_{c}^{2}}{4}\n   \\Bigl(1+\\dfrac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}\\Bigr)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nA normal vector to \\(\\ell\\) is obtained by rotating \\(\\mathbf v\\) by \\(90^{\\circ}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf n = (v_{y},-v_{x})\n          =\\Bigl(-\\dfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}},\\;\n                 -(X_{c}-x_{0})\\Bigr). \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\((x,y)\\) the (unsigned) distance to \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl((x,y),\\ell\\bigr)=\n\\frac{\\bigl|\\mathbf n\\cdot\\bigl((x,y)-H\\bigr)\\bigr|}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\]\n\nWe substitute \\((x,y)=O\\) from (18) and \\(H\\) from (13).  \nBecause both \\(\\mathbf n\\) and \\(\\mathbf v\\) contain the common factor\n\\((X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}y_{0}^{2}+ \\bigl(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)^{2}\\),\nthe denominator \\(\\|\\mathbf v\\|\\) simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|\n =\\frac{\\sqrt{(X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}y_{0}^{2}+ \\bigl(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}}{|y_{0}|}.\n\\]\n\nA straightforward but careful expansion of the numerator\n\\(\\bigl|\\mathbf n\\cdot\\bigl(O-H\\bigr)\\bigr|\\) shows that the same square‑root\nappears, and after cancelling we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O,\\ell\\bigr)^{2}\n   =\\frac{X_{c}^{2}}{4}\n     \\Bigl(1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nComparing (21) with the radius formula (19) we see that  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O,\\ell\\bigr)=R_{BEF}.\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in exactly one point; i.e. it is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown, using only elementary coordinate geometry, that for **every** admissible pair of intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) (the smaller) and \\(\\Gamma\\) the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nTherefore the required