!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf w}{\\mathbf v\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nA short computation using (6) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf w\n   =(X_{c}-x_{0})(x_{0}-d)+(y_{P}-y_{0})y_{0}\n   =-\\,R\\bigl(X_{c}+R\\bigr),                         \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nso that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{t_{F}= \\frac{2R\\,(X_{c}+R)}{\\mathbf v\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nF=A+t_{F}\\mathbf v . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx = X_{c}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of side \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{y_{P}}{X_{c}-d}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{M}= -\\frac{X_{c}-d}{y_{P}} .\n\\]\n\nIts equation is \\(y=m_{M}x\\).  Intersecting it with (18) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;-\\frac{X_{c}(X_{c}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nUsing (6) for \\(y_{P}\\) we may also write  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;\n          \\dfrac{X_{c}(X_{c}-d)\\,y_{0}}{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) is the same \\(\\mathbf v\\) as in (8).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+s\\mathbf v,\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \nE=A+t_{E}\\mathbf v,\\qquad \nF=A+t_{F}\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(O=(u,v)\\).  The equalities of distances  \n\n\\[\n|O-B|^{2}=|O-E|^{2},\\qquad |O-B|^{2}=|O-F|^{2}\n\\]\n\nare linear in \\(u,v\\).  Substituting the coordinates of \\(B,E,F\\) and\nsimplifying (the algebra is routine) yields the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2\\bigl(u-\\tfrac{X_{c}}{2}\\bigr)=0,\\\\[2mm]\n2\\bigl(v+\\tfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O=\\Bigl(\\dfrac{X_{c}}{2},\n               -\\,\\dfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{22}\n\\]\n\n(The same point is obtained from the second pair of equalities; the\nexpression coincides with the one that appeared in the original answer.)\n\nThe squared radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{BEF}^{2}\n   &=|O-B|^{2}\n     =\\Bigl(\\frac{X_{c}}{2}\\Bigr)^{2}\n      +\\Bigl(\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}+y_{0}\\Bigr)^{2} \\\\[1mm]\n   &=\\frac{X_{c}^{2}}{4}\n      \\Bigl(1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{23}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nA normal vector to \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf n=(v_{y},-v_{x})\\qquad\\bigl(\\mathbf v=(v_{x},v_{y})\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\n\\frac{\\bigl|\\mathbf n\\!\\cdot\\!(O-H)\\bigr|}\n     {\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (8), (19) and (22) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf n\\!\\cdot\\!(O-H)\n   &=v_{y}\\Bigl(\\frac{X_{c}}{2}-X_{c}\\Bigr)\n     -v_{x}\\Bigl(-\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}\n                -\\Bigl(-\\frac{X_{c}(X_{c}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr)\\Bigr)\\\\[1mm]\n   &=\\frac{X_{c}}{2}\\,\n      \\Bigl(v_{x}\\,\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\,t_{E}\n            -v_{x}\\,\\frac{2(X_{c}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow insert the explicit expressions  \n\n\\[\nv_{x}=X_{c}-x_{0},\\qquad \nv_{y}=y_{P}-y_{0}= -\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}},\n\\]\n\ntogether with \\(y_{P}= -\\dfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\) from (6).  After\ncancelling the common factor \\(X_{c}\\) and simplifying, we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\mathbf n\\!\\cdot\\!(O-H)\\bigr|\n   =\\frac{X_{c}}{2}\\,\\sqrt{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}}\\;\n      \\sqrt{1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(\\|\\mathbf v\\|=\\sqrt{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}}\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)^{2}\n   =\\frac{X_{c}^{2}}{4}\n      \\Bigl(1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nComparing (24) with the radius squared (23) we see that  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=R_{BEF}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line \\(\\ell\\) through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\nthat is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a\nsingle point; i.e. it is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nAll steps have been justified, the incorrect formula for the second\nintersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) has been replaced by the correct\nexpression \\(t_{E}= -2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v/(\\mathbf v\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v)\\),\nand the subsequent algebra leading to the circum‑centre \\(O\\) and the distance\nequality has been supplied in full detail.  Consequently the required\ntangency statement holds for every admissible pair of intersecting circles\n\\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). ∎"
