ces, for instance  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{BEF}^{2}&=|O-B|^{2}\\\\\n&=\\Bigl(\\frac{X_{c}}{2}\\Bigr)^{2}\n   +\\Bigl(\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}\\Bigr)^{2}\n   =\\frac{X_{c}^{2}}{4}\n     \\Bigl(1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(\\mathbf v=(v_{x},v_{y})\\) from (8).  \nA normal vector is \\(\\mathbf n=(v_{y},-v_{x})\\).  \nHence the (unsigned) distance from a point \\((x,y)\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl((x,y),\\ell\\bigr)=\n\\frac{\\bigl|\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\bigl((x,y)-H\\bigr)\\,\\bigr|}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\((x,y)=O\\) from (19) and \\(H\\) from (16).  \nBecause both \\(O\\) and \\(H\\) have the same abscissa \\(X_{c}\\), the scalar product simplifies dramatically:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf n\\cdot\\bigl(O-H\\bigr)=\nv_{y}\\bigl(O_{y}-H_{y}\\bigr)\n   =v_{y}\\Bigl(\n          -\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n          +\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}\n          -\\frac{(X_{c}-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\n        \\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(v_{y}= -\\dfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}}\\) and the definition of \\(t_{E}\\) (10) one obtains after straightforward algebra\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\mathbf n\\cdot(O-H)\\bigr|\n   =\\frac{|v_{x}|\\,X_{c}}{2}\n     \\sqrt{1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\|\\mathbf v\\|=|v_{x}|\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}}\\), the distance simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O,\\ell\\bigr)=\\frac{X_{c}}{2}\n       \\sqrt{1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}}}. \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nComparing (21) with the radius formula (20) we see that  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O,\\ell\\bigr)=R_{BEF}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the cir