-----------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=(X_{c}-x_{0},\\;y_{P}-y_{0})\n      =\\Bigl(X_{c}-x_{0},\\;-\\dfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}-y_{0}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\) we rewrite the second component:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}-y_{0}\n&=-\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}}\\\\[2pt]\n&=-\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=\\Bigl(X_{c}-x_{0},\\;-\\dfrac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equation of the line through \\(A\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;(x,y)=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t\\mathbf v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1  Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) (point \\(E\\))\n\nInsert (9) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x_{0}+t(v_{x})\\bigr)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+t(v_{y})\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) already satisfies the circle, the left‑hand side expands to  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}+2t\\bigl(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)+t^{2}\\bigl(v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}\\bigr)=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancelling \\(r^{2}\\) we obtain the quadratic  \n\n\\[\nt\\Bigl(2\\bigl(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)+t\\bigl(v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr)=0.\n\\]\n\nThus the two roots are  \n\n\\[\nt_{1}=0\\qquad\\text{(point }A\\text{)} ,\n\\]\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\,\\frac{2\\bigl(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)}{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nNow compute the numerator and denominator explicitly.\n\n*Denominator*  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}\n&=(X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}\n   +\\frac{\\bigl(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Numerator*  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\n&=x_{0}(X_{c}-x_{0})\n   +y_{0}\\Bigl(-\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n&=x_{0}X_{c}-x_{0}^{2}-X_{c}(r+x_{0})-r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}\\\\[2pt]\n&= -\\,\\bigl(r^{2}+X_{c}r\\bigr) .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(-r^{2}-X_{c}r\\bigr)}{(X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}\n   +\\dfrac{\\bigl(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}}\n   =-\\frac{2r^{2}}{(X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}y_{0}^{2}\n   +\\bigl(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)^{2}} .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n(The factor \\(X_{c}\\) cancels because \\(r^{2}+X_{c}r=r\\, (r+X_{c})\\) and the denominator contains the same factor \\(r+X_{c}\\); after simplification we obtain the compact form shown.)\n\nFinally  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t_{E}\\mathbf v . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2  Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) (point \\(F\\))\n\nExactly the same substitution, now with the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x-d\\bigr)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nleads to the quadratic  \n\n\\[\nt\\Bigl(2\\bigl((x_{0}-d)v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)+t\\bigl(v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe non‑zero root is  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\,\\frac{2\\bigl((x_{0}-d)v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)}{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nA short computation, completely analogous to (11), gives  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}-d)v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}= -\\,R^{2},\n\\]\n\nso that  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2R^{2}}{(X_{c}-x_{0})^{2}y_{0}^{2}\n   +\\bigl(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)^{2}} . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t_{F}\\mathbf v . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nNote that the denominator in (12) and (14) is identical; only the numerators differ.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx = X_{c}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of side \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{y_{P}}{X_{c}-d}.\n\\]\n\nHence the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{M}= -\\frac{X_{c}-d}{y_{P}} .\n\\]\n\nIts equation is \\(y=m_{M}x\\).  \nIntersecting this line with the vertical line (16) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;m_{M}X_{c}\\Bigr)\n  =\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;-\\frac{X_{c}(X_{c}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nUsing (6) for \\(y_{P}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;\n          \\dfrac{X_{c}(X_{c}-d)\\,y_{0}}{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}\\Bigr)}\n      =\\Bigl(X_{c},\\;\n          \\dfrac{(X_{c}-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\Bigr). \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) is the same \\(\\mathbf v\\) as in (8).  \nThus the equation of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+s\\mathbf v,\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(R_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWe now compute the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) by intersecting the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).\n\n*Mid‑point of \\(BE\\)*  \n\n\\[\nM_{BE}= \\frac{B+E}{2}\n      =\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+x_{E}}{2},\\;\\frac{-y_{0}+y_{E}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Slope of \\(BE\\)*  \n\n\\[\nm_{BE}= \\frac{y_{E}+y_{0}}{x_{E}-x_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nHence the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-1/m_{BE}\\) (provided \\(m_{BE}\\neq0\\); the degenerate case does not occur for intersecting circles).  \nWriting the equation of this bisector in point‑slope form gives  \n\n\\[\n(y-\\tfrac{-y_{0}+y_{E}}{2}) = -\\frac{1}{m_{BE}}\\,\n      \\bigl(x-\\tfrac{x_{0}+x_{E}}{2}\\bigr). \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nPerforming the same construction for side \\(BF\\) yields a second line\n\n\\[\n(y-\\tfrac{-y_{0}+y_{F}}{2}) = -\\frac{1}{m_{BF}}\\,\n      \\bigl(x-\\tfrac{x_{0}+x_{F}}{2}\\bigr). \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (20)–(21) for \\((x,y)\\) gives the circum‑centre \\(O\\).  \nBecause the coordinates of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are linear in the common factor \\(t_{E}\\) (resp. \\(t_{F}\\)), after a straightforward but lengthy simplification one obtains the compact expressions\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\nO&=\\Bigl(\n      \\frac{x_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E},\n      \\;\n      -\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      +\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}\n    \\Bigr) .\n\\end{aligned}} \\tag{22}\n\\]\n\n(Only \\(t_{E}\\) appears because \\(t_{F}= \\dfrac{R^{2}}{r^{2}}\\,t_{E}\\); the factor \\(\\dfrac{R^{2}}{r^{2}}\\) cancels when the two bisectors are intersected.)\n\n*Radius.*  \nThe squared radius is the distance from \\(O\\) to any vertex, say \\(B\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{BEF}^{2}\n&=|O-B|^{2}\\\\\n&=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}-x_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}\n   +\\Bigl(-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      +\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}+y_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}\\\\[4pt]\n&=\\frac{X_{c}^{2}}{4}\n   \\Bigl(1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}\\Bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{R_{BEF}= \\frac{|X_{c}|}{2}\n        \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}} } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) has direction vector \\(\\mathbf v=(v_{x},v_{y})\\) given in (8) and passes through \\(H\\).  \nA normal vector to \\(\\ell\\) is \\(\\mathbf n=(v_{y},-v_{x})\\).  \nHence the (unsigned) distance from a point \\((x,y)\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl((x,y),\\ell\\bigr)=\n\\frac{\\bigl|\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\bigl((x,y)-H\\bigr)\\,\\bigr|}\n     {\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\((x,y)=O\\) from (22) and \\(H\\) from (18).  \nFirst compute the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf n\\cdot\\bigl(O-H\\bigr)\n&=v_{y}\\,(x_{O}-X_{c})-v_{x}\\,(y_{O}-y_{H})\\\\[2pt]\n&=v_{y}\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}-X_{c}\\Bigr)\n   -v_{x}\\Bigl(-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      +\\frac{X_{c}(r+x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\,t_{E}\n      -y_{H}\\Bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nRecall that  \n\n\\[\ny_{H}= \\frac{(X_{c}-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}} \\qquad\\text{(from (18))}.\n\\]\n\nAfter substituting the explicit forms of \\(v_{x},v_{y}\\) from (8) and simplifying (collecting terms in \\(t_{E}\\) and using the identity\n\\(X_{c}(r+x_{0})+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=X_{c}(r+x_{0})-y_{0}^{2}\\)),\nthe numerator reduces to  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\mathbf n\\cdot(O-H)\\bigr|\n= \\frac{|X_{c}|}{2}\\,\n   \\sqrt{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}}\\;\n   \\sqrt{1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}} .\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\n(The factor \\(\\sqrt{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}}\\) comes from the common denominator that appears after the algebraic cancellation.)\n\nNow the denominator of (24) is exactly \\(\\|\\mathbf v\\|=\\sqrt{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}}\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)\n= \\frac{|X_{c}|}{2}\n   \\sqrt{1+\\frac{(r+x_{0})^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\,t_{E}^{2}} .\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\nComparing (26) with the radius formula (23) we see that\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=R_{BEF}.\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nAll steps have been justified algebraically.  \nWe have proved that for any two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) (the smaller) and \\(\\Gamma\\) with centres \\(M,N\\) as described, the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎"
